INT. SUBBASS SOUND - STUDIO B - DAY

CLOSE ON a mixing console, faders crawling up and down by themselves. Pull back to find Mo Delgado, 30s, producer, hoodie, three coffees deep, chewing a pen cap.

Through the glass, in the vocal booth, Linus Thorne, 50s, a former one-hit wonder in a leather vest, stands before a mic with his eyes closed, singing very gently about a sandwich.

LINUS (V.O.)
(through talkback, sincere)
"Oh, ham, you were never mine..."

Mo presses the talkback button.

MO
That's great, Linus. Another take. Maybe less ham.

Wren Okoye, 20s, the engineer, slumped at the second chair with a laptop, doesn't look up.

WREN
(to the room)
We've done forty-one takes.

MO
(muttering, to himself)
Forty-two, with feeling. Forty-two.

Mo's phone lights up on the desk.

ANGLE ON PHONE SCREEN

"LABEL - DENISE".

Mo answers on speaker.

DENISE (V.O.)
Mo. Tell me the single is done.

MO
It's, uh, ripening.

DENISE (V.O.)
It's a song about lunch meat. Is it done.

Wren mouths "No" at the phone. Mo waves her off.

MO
(to everyone, bright)
Okay team! Quick huddle, we're gonna just, vibe.

Priya Ramanathan, 20s, the assistant, enters with a tray of tea, nods at nobody, sets it down, and sits.

WE SEE Linus through the glass, tapping the window, mouthing something. Everyone ignores him.

PRIYA
(to Mo only)
He's asking if the lunch meat is "emotionally honest" enough.

MO
(to Priya)
Tell him it's the most honest ham I've ever heard.

BACK TO SCENE

WREN
(to herself, typing)
I'm updating my resume.

DENISE (V.O.)
I heard that.

Mo sinks into his chair. Nobody speaks. The faders keep creeping. Take forty-three begins, soft and sincere and terrible.
